<script>
export default {
  name: 'RagflowPipelinePage',
  data() {
    return {
      openStage: null,
      openResearch: null,
      linked: false,
      stages: [
        {
          key: 'pdf',
          name: 'Test document and acceptance criteria',
          body: 'I created a five-page test document that placed every known failure mode in one controlled input: merged cells, a table split across pages, a paragraph interrupted by a page break, notes cited on one page and defined on another, and a flowchart. This document was a test fixture for development, not part of the production pipeline. It let me check every requirement after each change.'
        },
        {
          key: 'mineru',
          name: 'Reading the PDF',
          body: 'MinerU converts the PDF into structured text and tables. In the test document, it preserves cells spanning multiple rows or columns, reconnects a table divided by a page boundary, and restores a paragraph interrupted by a page break. This provides a structured base for the custom repairs that follow.'
        },
        {
          key: 'notes',
          name: 'Connecting notes to their citations',
          body: 'Some sentences ended with a reference such as (4), while the corresponding Note (4) appeared several pages later. I wrote a rule-based script that located the matching note, repaired cases where the parser had split it, and copied it beside the sentence that cited it. The standalone copy was then removed so the document contained one complete version rather than two disconnected fragments.',
          rules: [
            'Detect a citation such as (4) at the end of a sentence.',
            'Locate the matching Note (4) or Remark (4) elsewhere in the relevant section.',
            'Repair cases where the parser has divided one note into multiple fragments.',
            'Normalise small inconsistencies in the parsed note labels.',
            'Copy the note beside the sentence that cites it, then remove the duplicated standalone copy.'
          ]
        },
        {
          key: 'mermaid',
          name: 'Flowchart to Mermaid',
          body: 'Flowcharts contained useful decision logic, but an image alone could not be searched reliably. I represented each diagram in Mermaid, a text format that preserves its nodes, branches, and labels. The same source can be rendered back into a diagram for readers and embedded as text for retrieval.'
        },
        {
          key: 'chunking',
          name: 'Chunking',
          body: 'RAGFlow divides the processed document into the units returned by retrieval. Because cross-page notes, tables, and paragraphs are repaired first, each chunk can contain a complete rule rather than one half of a relationship. The goal is not simply to create smaller passages, but to create passages that can support an answer on their own.'
        },
        {
          key: 'embedding',
          name: 'Embedding',
          body: 'Each chunk is converted into a vector representation so that a query can match it by meaning rather than exact wording. Mermaid diagrams enter this stage as text, which allows their decision logic to be retrieved alongside ordinary paragraphs and tables.'
        },
        {
          key: 'retrieval',
          name: 'Retrieval',
          body: 'The query is embedded with the same model and compared with the document chunks. In the test case, the linked sentence-and-note chunk becomes the top result for the question it answers. This provides an end-to-end check that the earlier structural repairs survive into retrieval.'
        },
        {
          key: 'generation',
          name: 'Generation',
          body: 'The retrieved chunks are handed to a generator (DeepSeek V4-flash), which writes the answer from the retrieved content.'
        }
      ],
      research: [
        {
          title: 'Baidu Unlimited OCR',
          body: 'I evaluated Baidu Unlimited OCR because its multi-page mode appeared capable of handling relationships across pages. On difficult public government documents, it preserved page-level layout reasonably well but did not recover the cross-page links required here. The multi-page mode also used a lower-detail configuration, and the available description indicated that its multi-page training data had been formed by concatenating individual pages. It was therefore useful for extraction, but not a replacement for the explicit linking step.'
        },
        {
          title: 'Visual and multimodal embedding',
          body: 'I also tested image-based and multimodal alternatives. Embedding each page as an independent image still leaves cross-page notes disconnected from the sentences that cite them. In the RAGFlow path I evaluated, images were not embedded directly: a vision model first converted them to text, and that text was embedded. For these documents, explicitly reconstructing the relationships in text produced a more controllable result.'
        },
        {
          title: 'Flowchart variants',
          body: 'A flowchart went through three treatments. OCR kept the raw pixels but lost edges and reading order. Free-form VLM descriptions were inconsistent, working for one figure and failing another. A structured prompt that emits Mermaid preserved every node, edge, and label, and it was the only variant that answered both flowchart questions.'
        },
        {
          title: 'Design findings',
          body: 'Three design decisions followed from the tests. First, tables and paragraphs must be repaired before chunking, because chunking cannot recover structure that has already been split incorrectly. Second, note numbers cannot be matched globally: the same number may be reused in different sections, so the linker must consider position and surrounding context. Third, the target is a self-contained chunk, not merely a higher retrieval score. A useful chunk contains both the rule and the note that limits or explains it.'
        }
      ]
    };
  },
};
</script>

